* Design patterns
    - Design patterns are reusable solutions to common software design problems.

    They are:
    not libraries
    not frameworks
    not copy-paste code
    They are proven ways to structure code.

1. Creational Design Patterns
   - Creational patterns solve one main problem:
     How should objects be created ?
     Instead of creating objects directly everywhere using new, these patterns provide controlled, flexible, and maintainable ways to create objects.

     1. Singleton Pattern
        - Purpose : Ensure only one object of a class exists in the entire application.
        - Used when a single shared instance is needed.
        - Real-Life Example
            . Printer manager
            . Database connection manager
            . Logger
            . Spring Beans (default scope)
        - Ex : public class Singleton {

                   private static Singleton instance;

                   private Singleton() {
                   }

                   public static Singleton getInstance() {

                       if (instance == null) {
                           instance = new Singleton();
                       }

                       return instance;
                   }
               }

        - Problem It Solves
          Without Singleton:
            new DBConnection();
            new DBConnection();
            new DBConnection();

          This may:
            . waste memory
            . create inconsistent state
            . create multiple expensive resources

          Singleton ensures:
            . one shared object
            . centralized access

        - Spring Boot Example
            . Spring beans are Singleton by default.

              @Service
              public class UserService {
              }

            . Spring creates only one instance unless scope changes.

        - Drawbacks
            . Harder to test
            . Global state
            . Thread safety issues if implemented poorly

    2. Builder Pattern
        - Purpose : Create complex objects step by step.
        - Useful when constructors become huge.
        - Problem Without Builder
            User user = new User(
                "Bhau",
                25,
                "Pune",
                "Java",
                "Spring Boot",
                true,
                false
            );
        - Problems:
            . hard to read
            . parameter order confusion
            . optional fields become messy
        - Builder Solution
            public class User {

                private String name;
                private int age;
                private String city;

                private User(UserBuilder builder) {
                    this.name = builder.name;
                    this.age = builder.age;
                    this.city = builder.city;
                }

                public static class UserBuilder {

                    private String name;
                    private int age;
                    private String city;

                    public UserBuilder setName(String name) {
                        this.name = name;
                        return this;
                    }

                    public UserBuilder setAge(int age) {
                        this.age = age;
                        return this;
                    }

                    public UserBuilder setCity(String city) {
                        this.city = city;
                        return this;
                    }

                    public User build() {
                        return new User(this);
                    }
                }
            }

        - Usage:
            User user = new User.UserBuilder()
                    .setName("Bhau")
                    .setAge(25)
                    .setCity("Pune")
                    .build();

        - Benefits
            . readable
            . flexible
            . avoids constructor explosion
            . immutable object support

        - Real Spring Example
            . RestTemplateBuilder
            . WebClient.Builder
            . UriComponentsBuilder

    3. Factory Pattern
        - Purpose : Create objects without exposing exact creation logic.
        - Instead of:
            . new Dog();
            . new Cat();
        - Use a factory.
        - Ex :
            Normal implementation
            Interface :
            public interface Animal {
                void sound();
            }

            Implementations :
            public class Dog implements Animal {
                public void sound() {
                    System.out.println("Bark");
                }
            }

            public class Cat implements Animal {
                public void sound() {
                    System.out.println("Meow");
                }
            }

            Factory
            public class AnimalFactory {

                public Animal getAnimal(String type) {

                    if(type.equals("DOG")) {
                        return new Dog();
                    }

                    if(type.equals("CAT")) {
                        return new Cat();
                    }

                    return null;
                }
            }

            Usage :
                AnimalFactory factory = new AnimalFactory();
                Animal animal = factory.getAnimal("DOG");
                animal.sound();

            - Benefits
                . hides object creation
                . loose coupling
                . easy extension

            - Spring Example
                . BeanFactory
                . ApplicationContext

    4. Abstract Factory Pattern
        - Purpose : Create families of related objects.
        - Factory creates one object.
        - Abstract Factory creates multiple related objects.
        - Real-Life Example
            . You build:
                Windows UI
                Mac UI

            . Each platform needs:
                Button
                Checkbox
                TextField

            . Windows family:
                WindowsButton
                WindowsCheckbox

            . Mac family:
                MacButton
                MacCheckbox

        - Structure

            - Without abstract factory pattern
            Product Interfaces
                interface Button {}
                interface Checkbox {}

            Concrete Products
                class WindowsButton implements Button {}
                class MacButton implements Button {}
                class WindowsCheckbox implements Checkbox {}
                class MacCheckbox implements Checkbox {}

            - With Abstract Factory
                interface GUIFactory {

                    Button createButton();

                    Checkbox createCheckbox();
                }

            Concrete Factories
                class WindowsFactory implements GUIFactory {

                    public Button createButton() {
                        return new WindowsButton();
                    }

                    public Checkbox createCheckbox() {
                        return new WindowsCheckbox();
                    }
                }

        - Benefits
            . ensures compatible object families
            . scalable
            . platform independence

        - Difference from Factory
            | Factory               | Abstract Factory             |
            | --------------------- | ---------------------------- |
            | Creates one product   | Creates families of products |
            | Simpler               | More complex                 |
            | One level abstraction | Multiple related objects     |

    5. Prototype Pattern
        - Purpose : Create new objects by cloning existing objects.
        - Useful when object creation is expensive.
        - Real-Life Example
            Creating a game character:
                . weapons
                . powers
                . skins
                . stats

            Instead of rebuilding:
            clone existing character
        - Example :
            public class Employee implements Cloneable {

                String name;

                public Employee(String name) {
                    this.name = name;
                }

                protected Object clone() throws CloneNotSupportedException {
                    return super.clone();
                }
            }

            - Usage
            Employee emp1 = new Employee("Bhau");

            Employee emp2 = (Employee) emp1.clone();

            System.out.println(emp1.name);
            System.out.println(emp2.name);

        - Benefits
            . faster creation
            . avoids repeated initialization
            . useful for expensive objects

        - Important Concept
          Shallow Copy vs Deep Copy
            Shallow Copy : Copies references.
            Deep Copy : Copies actual nested objects.

    - Summary
        | Pattern          | Main Goal                         |
        | ---------------- | --------------------------------- |
        | Singleton        | One object only                   |
        | Builder          | Build complex object step by step |
        | Factory          | Centralized object creation       |
        | Abstract Factory | Create related object families    |
        | Prototype        | Clone existing objects            |


